# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# Source material for the generated assets. The bulk of it is ~400 MB of
# original Gemini renders, which git is the wrong place for —
# scripts/case-images/resize.js reads assets/case-study-sources/ to regenerate
# public/images/case/, so keep that folder backed up somewhere off this machine.
#
# The two files below are the exceptions: a few hundred KB each, and neither can
# be recreated from anything else in the repo. Losing them would cost far more
# than they weigh, so they are kept.
# Source material lives in /assets. The originals the case-study images are
# exported from are kept in the repo so a fresh clone can regenerate them —
# about 200 MB, which is worth it against having them on one machine only.
#
# What stays out is only what is redundant or dead: the zip is byte-for-byte the
# same 106 files as the folder beside it, the backup folder holds the
# placeholders these images replaced, and the vtracer file is a bad auto-trace
# of the logo that the supplied vector superseded.
#
# Note the trailing /* rather than a bare /assets/: git will not descend into an
# ignored directory, so a plain directory rule cannot be re-opened selectively.
/assets/*
!/assets/case-study-sources/
!/assets/brand/
/assets/brand/*
!/assets/brand/logo-source-vector.svg
